        "retention_score": "last_access + 20 * log1p(frequency) * log1p(depth) + 200 / (1 + abs(depth - 4))",
        "rationale": "Depth-4 prefix blocks are treated as costly anchors whose eviction multiplies recomputation across many downstream blocks, so they receive a bounded structural bonus via 200/(1+abs(depth-4)). Frequency contributes only multiplicatively with depth, meaning frequency boosts retention primarily for deeper blocks (where repeated reuse suggests a shared long prefix) rather than shallow ones (which are cheap to recompute and closer to the root where eviction is unhelpful). Recency remains the dominant term at 100 per unit of time in normal ranges, preserving LRU's proven behavior while correcting its main failure mode: dropping long frequently-reused anchors that cost many extra tokens when recomputed. The multiplicative depth-frequency interaction naturally stays small for shallow blocks (depth 0 or 1), preventing the boost over-retention seen in the previous iteration where frequency added unconditionally and evicted hot anchors prematurely. Tie-breaking naturally falls back to last_access since it is the dominant additive term. Policy cost remains moderate: only log1p and one division, well within the 50000 us/request CPU feasibility budget given prior measurements of 2400us max."
